class:: WaveletEntropy
summary:: Normalized entropy of wavelet scale energies
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/WaveletBands

description::
Computes -sum(p*log2(p))/log2(levels+1), using the energy fraction p in each scale. Zero denotes concentration in one scale; silence is zero.

Uses an orthonormal discrete wavelet transform with Haar/db1 through db8 filters, following Daubechies, Orthonormal Bases of Compactly Supported Wavelets (1988), DOI 10.1002/cpa.3160410705. Each stage convolves and downsamples its approximation band; the high-pass filter is the quadrature mirror of the low-pass filter. Periodic mode transforms each frame directly. Symmetric mode transforms the doubled frame [x0, ..., xN-1, xN-1, ..., x0]; this is a redundant reflected-frame transform, not a conventional critically sampled symmetric-boundary DWT. No external wavelet library is linked.

No analysis window is applied. Periodic mode joins the last sample to the first; symmetric mode avoids that endpoint jump but can still contain slope discontinuities at the reflection. First output follows one full frame; results hold between hops. Silence returns zero. Higher orders increase filter support; symmetric mode doubles transform work and coefficient storage while keeping the same frame timing and output count. Memory is allocated at construction. Scale energies reveal multiscale structure; they should not be read as sharply separated frequency bands.

Vanilla FFT spectral analyzers describe uniform frequency bins. This family describes dyadic time/frequency scales with short filters at finer scales.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: in
Mono audio-rate input.
argument:: frameSize
Literal power of two from 32 through 8192. Default 1024; first output requires this many input samples.
argument:: order
Literal Daubechies order from 1 through 8; the filter has twice this many taps and this many vanishing moments. One is Haar.
argument:: levels
Literal number of decomposition levels, 1 through log2(frameSize). Default 6; outputs run from the coarsest approximation to finest detail.
argument:: hop
Literal fraction of frameSize between analyses, 0.0625 through 1, default 0.5.

argument:: family
Literal \db or \haar. Haar selects order one; db uses the requested order. Default db.
argument:: boundary
Literal \periodic (default) or \symmetric. Symmetric mode mirrors the frame including each endpoint and transforms 2 * frameSize coefficients. Scale energies divide by this doubled length, preserving input mean square. Other modes are rejected.

examples::
WaveletEntropy measures the disorder of energy distribution across dyadic wavelet scales (0 = concentrated in a single scale, 1 = uniform distribution across all multiscale bands). In this example, wavelet entropy extracted from an acoustic sound texture dynamically regulates the feedback decay and diffusion damping of a stereo comb delay network: low entropy keeps reflections focused, while high entropy opens into an expansive ambient cloud. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: plots real-time wavelet entropy.
code::
(
Routine({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("texture.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic evolving texture fallback
            var lfo = SinOsc.kr(0.15);
            XFade2.ar(SinOsc.ar([220, 330, 550]).sum * 0.1, PinkNoise.ar(0.2), lfo)
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var entropy = WaveletEntropy.kr(source, order: 8, boundary: \symmetric);
        var decay = entropy.lag(0.08).linexp(0.1, 0.9, 0.4, 3.5);
        var wet = CombC.ar(source ! 2, 0.2, [0.043, 0.057], decay) * 0.4;
        var sound = (source * 0.25 ! 2) + wet;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, entropy.clip(0, 1)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Wavelet Entropy", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 1.0, "norm");
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

